  "faithful": "The lower ranges of riders and trusses, which were brought on the upper surfaces of the floors and the futtocks, could have had little effect in preventing nothing beyond that there, which sense from the additional resistance they offered to compression, and the additional rigidity they pose to the structure in the event of grounding, or of being ashore. They certainly served as a firm base upon which to erect a series of riders with diagonally trusses, which were more advantageous by placed to afford efficient support to the extremities of the body. Yet the upper riders only seemed to the countries of the gun-decks, and therefore not very far above the line of non-action. Presuming, however, that this second series of riders was of considerable utility, as a base for the might probably have been obtained within out so much incumbance to the hold, and consequently without the objective being proved against it, which was to to the diagonal riders, of diminishing the stowage. According to the estimate made the IRS of Robert Seapings, the actual inclined contents of the diagonal frame were less than those of the ceiling which is superseded.",
